1. kube-api server (Front door & single source of truth)
Every command (kubectl, scheduler, controllers) talks only to this.
Reads/writes cluster state to etcd.
If API server is down → cluster is blind.
👉 Rule of thumb: Nothing bypasses the API server.

2. etcd (Cluster memory)
Key-value database.
Stores desired state: Pods, Deployments, ConfigMaps, Secrets.
If etcd is lost → cluster amnesia 😬.
👉 This is why etcd backups are non-negotiable in prod.

3. scheduler (Placement strategist)
Watches for Pending Pods.
Decides which worker node a Pod should run on.
Factors: CPU, memory, taints/tolerations, affinity.
👉 Scheduler does not run pods—it only assigns them.

4. controller manager (Autopilot)
Runs controllers like:
Node controller
ReplicaSet controller
Deployment controller
Constantly compares:
desired state vs actual state
If mismatch → corrective action.
👉 Example: Pod dies → controller creates a new one. Old-school ops, automated.


1. kubelet
Kubelet communicates with the API server, monitors Pods assigned to its node, 
ensures containers are running as expected, and reports node and Pod status back to the API server.

2. Container Runtime
Container Runtime (containerd or CRI-O) pulls container images and manages the container lifecycle, 
including creating, starting, and stopping containers.

3. kube-proxy
kube-proxy maintains network rules on worker nodes to route Service traffic to the appropriate backend Pods.

5. kubelet (Node supervisor)
Agent running on each worker node.
Talks to API server.
Responsibilities:
Pull Pod specs
Ask container runtime to start containers
Report health/status back
👉 kubelet = middle manager on each node.

6. Container Runtime (Actual worker)
Examples: containerd, CRI-O.
Pulls images.
Starts/stops containers.
👉 Kubernetes doesn’t run containers directly—runtime does.

7. kube-proxy (Networking traffic cop)
Manages networking rules (iptables / IPVS).
Enables:
Service → Pod communication
Load balancing across Pods
👉 Without kube-proxy, Services are just theory.

